<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>Understanding the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita (BNS), 2023 — Lex Factum & Partners</title><meta name="description" content="A plain-English explainer on the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023, its commencement, scope and practical reading points."><link rel="stylesheet" href="<?= base_url('styles.css') ?>"></head><body><div class="grain"></div><div class="cursor-ring"></div><div class="cursor-dot"></div><div class="progress"></div><header><div class="container nav-shell"><a class="brand" href="<?= site_url('/') ?>"><span class="brand-mark"><img src="<?= base_url('assets/lex-factum-logo-transparent.png') ?>" alt="Lex Factum & Partners"></span><span class="brand-copy"><strong>Lex Factum</strong><span>& PARTNERS · LAW FIRM</span></span></a><nav class="menu"><a href="<?= site_url('/') ?>">Home</a><a href="<?= site_url('about') ?>">About</a><a href="<?= site_url('practice-areas') ?>">Practice</a><a class="active" href="<?= site_url('updates') ?>">Updates</a><a href="<?= site_url('faq') ?>">FAQ</a><a href="<?= site_url('contact') ?>">Contact</a><a class="nav-cta" href="<?= site_url('appointment') ?>">Request Appointment ↗</a></nav><button class="burger" aria-label="menu">☰</button></div></header><main><section class="article-hero"><div class="container article-grid"><div class="reveal"><div class="eyebrow">BNS Explained · New Criminal Law</div><h1>Understanding the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita (BNS), 2023</h1><p class="article-dek">What it is, when it came into force, what it is designed to change, and how to read it alongside the new procedural and evidence laws.</p><div class="article-meta"><span>Lex Factum & Partners</span><span>Public information</span><span>Source-led explainer</span></div></div><div class="article-art glass-card reveal"><img src="<?= base_url('assets/lady-justice-bns.svg') ?>" alt="Lady Justice holding scales with Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita 2023 plaques"></div></div></section><section class="section light"><div class="container article-body"><div class="article-main"><div class="eyebrow">At a glance</div><h2>What is the BNS?</h2><p>The Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023 (Act No. 45 of 2023) is an Act to consolidate and amend the provisions relating to offences and matters connected with or incidental to them. India Code records its enactment date as 25 December 2023 and its enforcement date as 1 July 2024.</p><p>The commencement notification appointed 1 July 2024 as the date on which the provisions would come into force, except the provision contained in section 106(2).</p><div class="info-callout glass-light"><strong>Important distinction</strong><p>BNS is the substantive criminal-law statute. The Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita (BNSS), 2023 deals with criminal procedure, while the Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam (BSA), 2023 deals with evidence.</p></div><h2>Key themes to understand</h2><div class="article-list"><div><span>01</span><h3>New statutory framework</h3><p>The BNS replaces the Indian Penal Code framework for the provisions brought into force, with the new statute reorganising and amending offences and punishments.</p></div><div><span>02</span><h3>Modern offences and terminology</h3><p>The new law contains provisions addressing areas such as organised crime and terrorism, alongside revised terminology and offence structures.</p></div><div><span>03</span><h3>Different law, different procedure</h3><p>A criminal matter may involve reading the BNS together with the BNSS and BSA because the offence, procedure and evidentiary framework are distinct components of the legal system.</p></div><div><span>04</span><h3>Always verify the official text</h3><p>For a specific issue, the latest statutory text, notifications and court decisions should be checked before drawing legal conclusions.</p></div></div><h2>Why the change matters</h2><p>The transition is important because the governing statute for many criminal offences is no longer the IPC framework. It also matters for practitioners, investigators, courts, businesses and individuals because references in complaints, notices, charges and legal research may now point to BNS sections instead of IPC sections.</p><p>This explainer is a public-information guide, not a substitute for case-specific legal advice. The official statute and notifications should be consulted for precise wording, commencement and application.</p></div><aside class="article-aside"><div class="glass-light"><span>Official sources</span><h3>Check the statute, not a summary.</h3><p>India Code lists the BNS as Act No. 45 of 2023 and records its enforcement date as 1 July 2024.</p><a href="https://www.indiacode.nic.in/handle/123456789/20062?view_type=browse" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open India Code ↗</a></div><div class="glass-light"><span>Read next</span><h3>Other new criminal laws</h3><p>BNSS covers criminal procedure and BSA covers evidence. Together, the three statutes form the core of the new criminal-law framework.</p><a href="<?= site_url('updates') ?>">Back to Updates ↗</a></div></aside></div></section></main><footer><div class="container footer-grid"><div class="footer-brand"><a class="brand" href="<?= site_url('/') ?>"><span class="brand-mark"><img src="<?= base_url('assets/lex-factum-logo-transparent.png') ?>" alt="Lex Factum & Partners"></span><span class="brand-copy"><strong>Lex Factum</strong><span>& PARTNERS · LAW FIRM</span></span></a><p>Public legal information designed for clarity and source-led reading.</p></div><div><div class="footer-title">Explore</div><div class="footer-links"><a href="<?= site_url('practice-areas') ?>">Practice Areas</a><a href="<?= site_url('updates') ?>">Updates</a><a href="<?= site_url('faq') ?>">FAQ</a><a href="<?= site_url('contact') ?>">Contact</a></div></div><div><div class="footer-title">Practice</div><div class="footer-links"><a href="<?= site_url('criminal-cases') ?>">Criminal Cases</a><a href="